    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0416" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1659</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-07T14:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 7, 1915, 2 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1892. Practically all newspaper comment on note is friendly and shows
                    appreciation of the American position. The worst that is said <pb facs="0417"
                        n="159" xml:id="pg159"/> is that it is a discussion in a vacuum a sort of
                    lawyer’s brief. Every indication yet at hand shows that it is well received by
                    British press and public. I have yet heard no official opinion.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
